#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:186
msgid ""
"Active Directory and Samba distinguish between the following Active "
"Directory group types. The :program:`S4 Connector` synchronizes these "
"groups. In the LDAP directory service, the groups have attributes to "
"label the group types. However, the group types only have a meaning in "
"Active Directory. Nubus doesn't evaluate the group types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:192
msgid "Local"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:193
msgid ""
"*Local* groups exist only on a single host. The :program:`S4 Connector` "
"synchronizes local groups created in Samba. Therefore, they also appear "
"in the *Groups* management module in the *Management UI*. There is no "
"need to create local groups in the *Groups* management module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:198
msgid "Global"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:199
msgid ""
"*Global* groups are the default type when you create groups in the "
"*Groups* management module. A global group applies to one domain but can "
"accept members from other domains. If there is a trust relationship with "
"a domain, the *Groups* management module shows the groups from other "
"trusted domains and you can assign permissions to them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:209 ../../iam/group-management.rst:218
msgid ""
"Nubus for UCS doesn't support multiple domains or forests or outgoing "
"trust relationships."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:211
msgid "Domain local"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:212
msgid ""
"*Domain local* groups can also include members from other domains if "
"there's a trust relationship or if they're part of a forest. The *Groups*"
" management module shows the domain local groups only in their own "
"domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:220
msgid "Universal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:221
msgid ""
"*Universal* groups can adopt members from all domains and these members "
"are visible across all domains in a forest. The *global catalog* is a "
"separate segment of the Samba directory service and it stores universal "
"groups. The :program:`S4 Connector` doesn't synchronize the *global "
"catalog* to OpenLDAP."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:227
msgid "The Active Directory capability in Samba doesn't support domain forests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:229
msgid "You can apply the group types to the following kind of groups:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:231
msgid "Security groups"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:232
msgid ""
"Administrators use them to assign permissions, similar to user groups in "
"Nubus."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:235
msgid "Distribution groups"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:236
msgid "Active Directory uses them for mailing lists."
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:241
msgid "Group overlay module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../iam/group-management.rst:243
msgid ""
"Nubus only saves group membership properties in the group objects and not"
" in the respective user objects in the directory service. However, some "
"applications expect group membership properties at the user objects in "
"the attribute ``memberOf``. An overlay module in the OpenLDAP directory "
"server in Nubus allows presenting these attributes automatically based on"
" the group information. Nubus doesn't write the additional attributes to "
"the directory service. The directory service shows the attributes on-the-"
"fly through the overlay module when it answers a query for a user object."
msgstr ""

